philosophy
----------

Purpose

    The collector exists to gather evidence from web applications.

    It observes.

    It records.

    It preserves.

    It does not analyze.


Role

    The collector is a source of truth.

    It produces structured evidence for downstream tools.

    Interpretation belongs elsewhere.


Evidence

    Evidence is expensive.

    Reasoning is cheap.

    A collector may only have one opportunity to observe a web application
    in a particular state. Collected evidence should therefore be preserved
    whenever practical, allowing future analysis without repeating the
    crawl.


Collection

    Collect broadly.

    Discard nothing unnecessarily.

    Preserve provenance.

    Every collected artifact should retain enough information to understand
    where it came from and how it was discovered.


Analysis

    Analysis is intentionally separated from collection.

    The collector does not infer technologies, business logic, entities,
    vulnerabilities or other higher-level concepts.

    Downstream tools are responsible for interpretation.


Architecture

    Build small, focused modules.

    Give each module one responsibility.

    Modules communicate through explicit, structured data rather than
    in-memory coupling.

    Simplicity is preferred over abstraction.


State

    State should be explicit.

    Work queues, visited pages and crawl metadata should be visible and
    inspectable rather than hidden inside control flow.

    The current state of a crawl should be understandable at any point.


Determinism

    Given the same input and the same application state, the collector
    should strive to produce the same output.

    Collection should avoid unnecessary randomness.


Output

    Output should be structured.

    Output should be versioned.

    Output should be machine-readable.

    Output should remain practical for humans to inspect.

    JSONL is preferred where it naturally represents a stream of events.


Provenance

    Every observation should retain its origin.

    Pages, actions, artifacts and discoveries should be traceable back to
    the crawl that produced them.


Browser Automation

    The collector uses browser automation to observe applications as users
    experience them.

    It is not a browser test suite.

    It does not verify correctness.

    It collects evidence.


Scope

    The collector should remain focused on collection.

    Features that primarily perform analysis, enrichment or reporting belong
    in downstream tools.

    The collector should resist expanding beyond its role.


Evolution

    The collector should be easy to extend without changing its core
    architecture.

    New discovery mechanisms, artifact types and collection strategies
    should integrate naturally into the existing design.

    Growth should come from adding focused capabilities rather than
    increasing complexity.
